From 8d5033e5e427406ad94f308e0bdf20b228e8c844 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:42:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Add a setting for the starting permission mode of new
 Claude sessions

The Claude provider's session `permissionMode` is chosen per session and
always starts at the hardcoded `default` seed in `resolveChatConfig`, so a
user who always wants the same mode has to change it by hand in every new
session.

Add `chat.agentHost.claudeAgent.defaultPermissionMode`, mirrored to the agent
host as the `claudeDefaultPermissionMode` root config key through the existing
declarative `agentHost` configuration sync. `ClaudeAgent.resolveChatConfig`
reads it as the `validateOrDefault` fallback, the single point where a new
chat's initial mode is decided. A session that already carries a mode keeps
it: the restore path re-resolves with the persisted values, and
`validateOrDefault` gives a valid incoming value precedence over the fallback.

The setting is Claude-scoped rather than shared across providers because the
three providers do not share an approval axis. Copilot uses the platform
`autoApprove` key, Codex uses `codex.permissionsPreset`, and both value sets
are disjoint from Claude's `permissionMode`, so one cross-provider setting
would have to invent equivalences between modes the providers do not share.

Scope it APPLICATION_MACHINE rather than APPLICATION. Its location in the
agent-host settings family argues for APPLICATION, but the closer precedent is
by purpose: `chat.tools.global.autoApprove` and `chat.autoReply` are both
APPLICATION_MACHINE because they suppress confirmation prompts, and this
setting can carry `bypassPermissions`. Machine scope keeps the value tied to
the machine it was set on, so a remote window resolves and writes it against
the remote user settings, and it is excluded from profile export and import.

A configured mode that auto-approves tool calls (`acceptEdits`, `auto`,
`bypassPermissions`) degrades to `default` while managed policy restricts
auto-approval, matching how the sessions provider already clamps its
new-session approval seed.
