From 0381acc3a76a3402909c98829086a2ce2b6f9cb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 15:00:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Do not restore a persisted default permission mode, and
 honour it for workspace-less chats

Two review findings on the new
`chat.agentHost.claudeAgent.defaultPermissionMode` setting.

The mirrored root key can carry `bypassPermissions` but was missing from
`clientOwnedApprovalRootConfigKeys`, so `AgentConfigurationService` restored it
from `agent-host-config.json` on startup. That is the case the set exists to
prevent: the persisted value is a snapshot of one client's settings, and
reviving it re-grants an elevated mode that a setting or a policy may have
tightened while the host was stopped. The connected client re-pushes this key
on every connect and reconnect through the declarative `agentHost` sync, just
like the other members, so falling back to the schema default until it does is
the fail-safe direction. The policy clamp only guarded the live read, not this
one.

A workspace-less create supplies neither config nor working directories, so
`AgentService._resolveCreatedSessionConfig` returns early and the chat gets no
resolved session config. `_bindFreshConversation` then decided the mode through
`_resolvePermissionMode`, whose fallback was still a hardcoded `default`, so
those chats ignored the setting entirely. Route that fallback through the same
`_defaultPermissionMode` helper `resolveChatConfig` uses, so both creation
paths read the setting in one place and neither can diverge on the policy
clamp.
